///1TURBO
Turbo Pascal: editing, compiling, and running programs under CP/M-80.
This section describes the program workflow, not the Pascal language.
Menu labels and compiler options differ among Turbo Pascal releases.

///2DRIVES
The configured drives for this system are:

  A: CP/M system disk; read-only
  B: language.dsk; compiler and language files
  C: Utility.dsk; utility files
  D: not configured
  E: work1.dsk; writable source and output disk
  F: work2.dsk; writable second work disk

Use E: for source and compiled programs. Start Turbo Pascal from B:
with a drive-qualified command name. Drive prefixes select disks;
CP/M does not use folders. Filenames use the 8.3 form, for example
HELLO.PAS and HELLO.COM.

///2CHECKING
List the compiler and work disks:

  A>DIR B:
  A>DIR E:

Check the compiler name on B: and confirm the source is on E:.
TURBO.COM is a common launcher name, but packages can differ.
Do not save files on A:, because it is read-only.

///2STARTING
Select E: before starting the compiler. This makes E: the work disk.
The compiler program is loaded from drive B:.

  A>E:
  E>B:TURBO

If the installed program has another name, use that name instead.
Consult its manual if the command does not start the compiler.

///2LOADING
In Turbo Pascal, choose the Files or Load command. Enter the source
file on E:, including its drive and extension:

  E:HELLO.PAS

Edit the source in the environment. Save it as E:HELLO.PAS so it
remains on the work disk, not the compiler disk. Use the exact menu
commands and filename prompts shown by your release.

///2COMPILING
Choose the Compile command. If the environment asks for a destination,
choose disk output to create a program that can be run after leaving
Turbo Pascal. A compile-to-memory result may run only in the compiler
environment and may not create a .COM file.

Read all compiler messages. Correct errors, save HELLO.PAS on E:, and
compile again. After a successful disk compile, leave the compiler
and check the files:

  E>DIR HELLO.*

The usual standalone output is HELLO.COM. The output name or drive
can vary by release and compiler settings; use the reported result.

///2RUNNING
At the CP/M prompt, select E: and run the command file without typing
its .COM extension:

  A>E:
  E>HELLO

The program runs as a normal CP/M transient command. Keep any data
files on E: unless the program's documentation specifies another
drive. Use matching runtime or support files if the program asks for
them.

///2PROBLEMS
Bad command or compiler not found:
Check DIR B: and use the exact launcher name on that disk.

Source file not found:
Check DIR E:. Enter the full E:HELLO.PAS name in the Load command.

Compile succeeded but no .COM exists:
Choose disk output rather than memory output, then compile again.
Check the output name and destination with DIR E:.

Disk is read-only or a write failed:
Do not write to A:. Save source and output on writable E:.

Program is not found after compiling:
Check DIR E: for the actual output name, select E:, and run the name
without the .COM suffix.

